 The 2026-08-11 flight settled which step wedges: two rows painted, the
 eligible row and the read-only touch, and nothing below them. The box
 died inside e1000-reset, upstream of both arms this image existed to
 compare, so ASDE is untested rather than disproved and the reset is the
 subject now.

 That inverts the order. 82583V CTRL bit 5 -- "When set to 1b, the MAC
 ignores the speed indicated by the PHY ... This bit must be set to 0b in
 the 82583V" -- is still the question, and na-bring-up-after is already
 the whole of the bring-up except the reset. Firmware hands this part over
 with CTRL.SLU set (CTRL=0x180240 on that flight, SLU being bit 6), so
 both arms can be compared without pulsing RST at all. They run FIRST,
 and the reset rides LAST as ONE row, the control against 08-13. This
 paragraph said "split into the five MMIO operations e1000-reset performs"
 until 2026-08-20 and the code below has not done that for some time: the
 split existed to name which operation wedged, that has been answered twice,
 and `asde-reset-brief` says so ninety lines down. Two paragraphs in one
 chapter disagreeing about what the reset does is worse than either being
 wrong, because a reader who takes the first one never reaches the second --
 it was quoted as binding across lanes the day this was corrected.

 The 2026-08-10 flight of this probe returned nothing, and the reason was
 in this file: the mount was a PRECONDITION, so a volume that would not
 mount ended the flight before a single stage ran. It is not one now. The
 rows paint whether or not a volume is there, the markers are written when
 there happens to be one, and a stage's row goes down BEFORE the touch
 that might kill the machine. Two boots have established that a wedge
 leaves the glass intact (HardwareSitting, 2026-08-05: rows painted, then
 the machine stalled, rows still readable), so the glass is a bank for a
 person standing at the board, which is every sitting we have ever had.
 The 08-11 stick came back with nothing written, so the glass is the only
 bank that has ever worked here and the ordering above is what it buys.

 The markers remain because they cost nothing here: each is under one
 cluster, so it takes the small-file path and one 512-byte transfer, and
 cannot be defeated by the multi-cluster timeout WORKS-9 measured.

 We say:

Section: Marker Files

 An 8.3 directory name is eleven bytes with no dot: eight of name padded
 with spaces, then three of extension.

  asde-name : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  asde-name (buf) (digit) =
   let a = poke-byte buf 0 65
   in let s = poke-byte buf 1 83
   in let d = poke-byte buf 2 68
   in let e = poke-byte buf 3 69
   in let n = poke-byte buf 4 (48 + digit)
   in let p1 = poke-byte buf 5 32
   in let p2 = poke-byte buf 6 32
   in let p3 = poke-byte buf 7 32
   in let t = poke-byte buf 8 84
   in let x = poke-byte buf 9 88
   in poke-byte buf 10 84

 Text crosses to the wire as Unicode, not as the CCE the compiler holds
 internally, so every byte goes through to-unicode at this boundary.

  asde-bytes : Integer, Text, Integer -> Integer
  asde-bytes (buf) (s) (i) =
   if i >= text-length s then i
   else let b = poke-byte buf i (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i)))
   in asde-bytes buf s (i + 1)

 A marker is written when a volume happens to be mounted and skipped when
 one is not. It answers False in that case, which is the same answer a
 failed write gives, and the row on the glass is what carries the reading
 either way.

  asde-mark : GfatMount, Integer, Text -> [Device.Port] Boolean
  asde-mark (m) (digit) (body) =
   if (m.gm-ok) == False then False
   else let nb = alloc-zeroed 16 16
   in let nm = asde-name nb digit
   in let n = text-length body
   in let buf = alloc-zeroed 512 64
   in let w = asde-bytes buf body 0
   in gfat-write-file (m.gm-vol) nb buf n

 GopFat16 records WHICH step of the mount failed in diag cell 80, at every
 exit, and always has. The 2026-08-10 flight collapsed all seven of those
 into one line reading "no ESP", which named none of them and cost a
 sitting. The cell is free to read and it is read here.

  asde-stage-name : Integer -> Text
  asde-stage-name (s) =
   if s == gfat-stage-hdr then "GPT header read failed"
   else if s == gfat-stage-sig then "no EFI PART signature"
   else if s == gfat-stage-ents then "entry array read failed"
   else if s == gfat-stage-guid then "no ESP GUID in the entry array"
   else if s == gfat-stage-bpb then "BPB sector read failed"
   else if s == gfat-stage-bps then "bytes-per-sector not 512"
   else if s == gfat-stage-ok then "ok"
   else "not asked"

  asde-bank-line : GfatMount, Boolean -> Text
  asde-bank-line (m) (wrote) =
   if m.gm-ok then "bank live, ASDE0.TXT written=" & (if wrote then "y" else "n")
   else "no bank, mount stage " & show (xdiag-get gfat-cell-stage)
        & " (" & asde-stage-name (xdiag-get gfat-cell-stage)
        & ") -- the rows below are the reading"

Section: Rows

  arow : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  arow (base) (stride) (font) (y) (scale) (s) (col) (w) =
   let lines = gop-draw-text-wrap base stride font 40 y s col scale w
   in y + lines * 16 * scale + 6

Section: The SLU Discriminator

 The 08-13 arms and the ASDE readback together leave one question open and
 it is the one that matters before B2c. Every CTRL bit this probe has ever
 written is a bit that cannot report whether the WRITE took: SLU was
 already set by firmware, ASDE does not stick, and RST self-clears, so
 "never written" and "written and completed" both read as settled=1. If
 CTRL is not writable by us at all then the link came up over MDIO and the
 CTRL write contributes nothing -- which matters because RX/TX bring-up
 writes RCTL, TCTL and the ring registers, and a silent CSR write path
 would surface there as a receive path that never receives.

 So: clear a bit that IS currently set, and read it back. SLU is the only
 one available, and s2 is the whole experiment -- if SLU still reads as set
 immediately after we cleared it, CTRL is read-only to us and no link
 behaviour is needed to know it. s3 then asks the slower question of
 whether the MAC's link view follows. s4 restores the register.

  asde-clear-slu : Integer = #FFFFFFFF - e1000-ctrl-slu

  asde-slu-line : Text, Integer, Integer -> Text
  asde-slu-line (label) (ctrl) (status) =
   let a = label & " CTRL=" & show ctrl
   in let b = a & " SLUbit=" & na-yn (bit-and ctrl e1000-ctrl-slu)
   in let c = b & " STATUS=" & show status
   in c & " LU=" & na-yn (bit-and status na-lu)

  asde-slu-test : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  asde-slu-test (base) (stride) (font) (scale) (y0) (w) (mmio) =
   let c0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let s0 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
   in let y1 = arow base stride font y0 scale (asde-slu-line "SLU s1 before:" c0 s0) 9474192 w
   in let wclr = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl (bit-and c0 asde-clear-slu)
   in let c1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let s1 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
   in let y2 = arow base stride font y1 scale (asde-slu-line "SLU s2 cleared:" c1 s1) 16776960 w
   in asde-slu-after base stride font scale y2 w mmio c0

  asde-slu-after : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  asde-slu-after (base) (stride) (font) (scale) (y2) (w) (mmio) (c0) =
   let st = e1000-settle-mdio mmio
   in let c2 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let s2 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
   in let y3 = arow base stride font y2 scale (asde-slu-line "SLU s3 after 10ms:" c2 s2) 16776960 w
   in let wr = e1000-write mmio e1000-reg-ctrl c0
   in let st2 = e1000-settle-mdio mmio
   in let c3 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let s3 = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-status
   in arow base stride font y3 scale (asde-slu-line "SLU s4 restored:" c3 s3) 65280 w

Section: Stages

 Stage 2 is the read-only touch, stages 3 and 4 are the two arms with no
 reset, and the staged reset is stages 5 to 9. Each row is painted BEFORE
 the touch that follows it, so the last row on the glass names the last
 thing that completed and the first thing that did not.

 The reset is now ONE row, not five. The five-stage split existed to name
 which MMIO operation wedged the box, and it has been answered twice: a
 warm reset completes on this part. It is kept here only as a control
 against the 08-13 reading, and it costs two rows instead of seven because
 the glass has run out of room -- at 1280 wide the font scales to 2, so a
 row is 38 pixels and only about twenty fit. The staged version is in the
 depot history if a wedge ever comes back.

  asde-reset-brief : Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  asde-reset-brief (base) (stride) (font) (scale) (y0) (w) (mmio) =
   let y1 = arow base stride font y0 scale "RESET: warm reset, the control for 08-13" 16755200 w
   in let settled = e1000-reset mmio
   in let t = na-touch mmio
   in let a = "RESET done: settled=" & show settled & " CTRL=" & show (t.nt-ctrl)
   in arow base stride font y1 scale (a & " STATUS=" & show (t.nt-status)) 65280 w
 The 2026-08-13 flight returned IDENTICAL values from both arms where the
 bed has them differing sharply, and could not say why, because three
 explanations survived it: ASDE does nothing on this part, the ASDE write
 does not stick, or the second arm inherited a link the first had already
 established. Two of those are closed here.

 CTRL is read back after each arm and printed, so a bit that does not
 stick is visible rather than inferred. And ASDE=1 runs FIRST, from the
 state firmware left, so the arm that sets the bit is the one with nothing
 ahead of it -- on 08-13 it ran second and its reading could always have
 been the first arm's link showing through.

  asde-arm-line : Text, NicAsdeArm, Integer -> Text
  asde-arm-line (label) (arm) (ctrl) =
   let a = na-line label arm
   in let b = a & " CTRLback=" & show ctrl
   in b & " ASDEbit=" & na-yn (bit-and ctrl e1000-ctrl-asde)

  asde-arms : GfatMount, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Integer
  asde-arms (m) (base) (stride) (font) (scale) (y3) (w) (mmio) (dev-id) =
   let y4 = arow base stride font y3 scale "ASDE: no-reset arms, ASDE=1 FIRST from the firmware state" 9474192 w
   in let on = na-bring-up-after mmio dev-id True
   in let onctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
   in let y5 = arow base stride font y4 scale (asde-arm-line "ASDE=1 first" on onctrl) 65280 w
   in act
    m3 <- asde-mark m 3 ("stage3 asde1 first " & na-line "ASDE=1" on)
    let off = na-bring-up-after mmio dev-id False
    in let offctrl = e1000-read mmio e1000-reg-ctrl
    in let y6 = arow base stride font y5 scale (asde-arm-line "ASDE=0 second" off offctrl) 65280 w
    in act
      m4 <- asde-mark m 4 ("stage4 asde0 second " & na-line "ASDE=0" off)
      y7 <- asde-slu-test base stride font scale y6 w mmio
      asde-reset-brief base stride font scale y7 w mmio
    end
   end

  asde-armed : GfatMount, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer, PciDevice -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Integer
  asde-armed (m) (base) (stride) (font) (scale) (y1) (w) (dev) =
   let y2 = arow base stride font y1 scale ("ASDE: eligible at " & show (dev.pci-bus) & ":" & show (dev.pci-dev) & "." & show (dev.pci-func) & " -- entering bring-up") 16776960 w
   in let mmio = na-mmio-of dev
   in act
    m1 <- asde-mark m 1 ("stage1 eligible mmio=" & show mmio)
    let touch = na-touch mmio
    in let y3 = arow base stride font y2 scale (na-touch-line mmio touch) 16776960 w
    in act
      m2 <- asde-mark m 2 ("stage2 touch STATUS=" & show (touch.nt-status) & " CTRL=" & show (touch.nt-ctrl))
      asde-arms m base stride font scale y3 w mmio (dev.pci-device-id)
    end
   end

  asde-stages : GfatMount, Integer, Integer, CbfFont, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Integer
  asde-stages (m) (base) (stride) (font) (scale) (y0) (w) =
   let y1 = arow base stride font y0 scale "ASDE: scanning bus 0" 9474192 w
   in let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
   in when e1000-find scan
    is None -> act
      mk <- asde-mark m 1 "stage1 no Intel NIC on bus 0 (scope: bus 0 only)"
      arow base stride font y1 scale "ASDE: no Intel NIC on bus 0 (scope: bus 0 only)" 16755200 w
    end
    is Just (dev) ->
      if na-eligible dev == False then act
        mk <- asde-mark m 1 ("stage1 rejected " & na-reject-line dev)
        arow base stride font y1 scale (na-reject-line dev) 16755200 w
      end
      else asde-armed m base stride font scale y1 w dev

Section: Entry

  opening : [Device.Port, Device.Mmio] Integer
  opening =
   let base = boot-fb-base 0
   in let w = boot-fb-w 0
   in let h = boot-fb-h 0
   in let stride = boot-fb-stride 0
   in let font = cbf-init
   in let scale = if w >= 1024 then 2 else 1
   in act
    ri <- runtime-init 0
    att <- usb-attach
    let bg = gop-fill-rect base stride 0 0 w h 1710638
    in let y0 = arow base stride font 40 scale "ASDE STAGE PROBE -- B2: does a CTRL write reach this part at all?" 65535 w
    in act
      m <- gfat-mount-esp
      m0 <- asde-mark m 0 "stage0 mounted, write path live"
      let y1 = arow base stride font y0 scale (asde-bank-line m m0) (if m.gm-ok then 9474192 else 16755200) w
      in act
        y2 <- asde-stages m base stride font scale y1 w
        shot-wait base w h stride font (att.ua-kbd)
      end
    end
   end